c.setTitle('Homework 2 Basic Video Processing');page=0;y=0

def start(title):
 global page,y
 if page:c.showPage()
 page+=1;y=732
 c.setFillColor(black);c.setFont('Helvetica-Bold',17);c.drawString(48,y,title);y-=30
 c.setFont('Helvetica',9);c.drawString(48,30,'CSI 471/571 | Homework 2');c.drawRightString(564,30,str(page))

def p(t,size=10.5):
 global y
 c.setFont('Helvetica',size)
 for line in textwrap.wrap(t,width=int(91*10.5/size)):
  c.drawString(48,y,line);y-=14
 y-=8

def heading(t):
 global y
 c.setFont('Helvetica-Bold',12);c.drawString(48,y,t);y-=20

def grid(items,side=145):
 global y
 cols=3
 for i,(label,path) in enumerate(items):
  row,col=divmod(i,cols);xx=48+col*174;top=y-row*(side+30)
  c.setFont('Helvetica',9);c.drawString(xx,top,label)
  c.drawImage(str(path),xx,top-side-8,width=side,height=side,preserveAspectRatio=True,anchor='c')
 y-=((len(items)+2)//3)*(side+30)+8

start('Homework 2 Basic Video Processing')
p('This report presents image filtering, direct array-based shape drawing, consecutive-frame subtraction, and a nine-level Haar wavelet transform with exact reconstruction. All image results are generated by the included Python source.')
p('Author: Anirudh Amuda. All four computational tasks have been executed and the generated results verified.' if 'input_frames' in m['problem3'] else 'Submission status: Problems 1, 2, and 4 have been executed and checked. Problem 3 requires a student-recorded video; the video pipeline is implemented and tested, but no personal recording was supplied. This report must be regenerated after that recording is processed.')
p('Human collaborators: none reported. Assistance: OpenAI ChatGPT assisted with implementation, explanations, and verification.')
heading('Problem 1 Convolution using kernels')
p('Input: the original 512 x 512 grayscale Lena image extracted from the supplied assignment document. Computation uses float64, so negative derivative values and fractional filter responses are preserved. The output remains 512 x 512. Outside-image samples are zero, as requested.')
p('The convolve function implements true convolution by reversing the kernel in both axes. For an even-sized kernel its anchor is floor(size/2); this agrees with same-sized mathematical convolution. Median filtering is nonlinear, so it uses a neighborhood median rather than a convolution sum.')
p('Smoothing images retain their original intensity scale. Signed derivatives, gradient magnitudes, Laplacian, and DoG are displayed using min-max normalization: display = 255*(value-min)/(max-min). A constant response maps to zero. Raw float64 arrays are also saved as .npy files; display normalization never changes the calculations.')
heading('Exact Gaussian kernels')
p('3 x 3: [[1,2,1], [2,4,2], [1,2,1]] / 16.')
p('5 x 5: [[1,4,7,4,1], [4,16,26,16,4], [7,26,41,26,7], [4,16,26,16,4], [1,4,7,4,1]] / 273. This is the matrix in the supplied figure.')
p('Zero padding causes visible border darkening in the smoothing outputs and border responses in derivative outputs. These are expected consequences of the specified boundary rule, rather than scene edges.')

start('Problem 1 Smoothing and first derivatives')
grid([(s,R/'problem1'/f'{f}.png') for s,f in [('Original','original'),('1 Median 3 x 3','median'),('2 Gaussian 3 x 3','gaussian3'),('3 Gaussian 5 x 5','gaussian5'),('4 Roberts magnitude','roberts_magnitude'),('5 Prewitt magnitude','prewitt_magnitude')]])
p('1. Median: replaces each pixel with the median of nine neighborhood samples. It suppresses isolated outliers while preserving many sharp boundaries better than averaging; small image details can still disappear.')
p('2. Gaussian 3 x 3: a weighted local average that reduces high-frequency variation. Hair and feather texture become slightly softer, while major boundaries remain visible.')
p('3. Gaussian 5 x 5: averages over a larger neighborhood and produces stronger smoothing than the 3 x 3 kernel. Fine texture is reduced further.')
p('4. Roberts: uses exactly the assigned [-1,1] and vertical [-1;1] kernels, even though the classic Roberts cross operator uses diagonal 2 x 2 kernels. Adjacent-pixel differences reveal edges and remain sensitive to fine texture.')
p('5. Prewitt: the assigned x and y kernels average across the perpendicular direction while differentiating in the other. The magnitude combines both directions using sqrt(gx*gx+gy*gy).')

start('Problem 1 Sobel Laplacian and DoG')
grid([(s,R/'problem1'/f'{f}.png') for s,f in [('6 Sobel magnitude','sobel_magnitude'),('7 Laplacian','laplacian'),('8 Gaussian5 - Gaussian3','dog')]],side=145)
p('6. Sobel: uses [-1,0,1; -2,0,2; -1,0,1] and its perpendicular counterpart. The greater weight on the central row or column adds smoothing compared with Prewitt. Major contours are strong, although texture and padding borders also respond.')
p('7. Laplacian: [0,1,0; 1,-4,1; 0,1,0] adds the horizontal and vertical second differences. The horizontal term is (I[x+1]-I[x])-(I[x]-I[x-1]); the vertical term has the same form. Thus it is literally differences of first differences. It is zero on constant interior regions, responds to rapid intensity curvature, and amplifies noise.')
p('8. DoG: the 5 x 5 Gaussian output minus the 3 x 3 Gaussian output is computed before any rounding. Its signed range is then normalized to [0,255]. The result emphasizes boundaries and some fine-scale texture while suppressing the common low-frequency image content.')
p('A difference of two Gaussian smoothings approximates a scaled Laplacian of Gaussian when their scales are related. In the continuous model, changing Gaussian scale is proportional to its spatial Laplacian. These small discrete kernels therefore give a qualitative approximation, not an exact LoG. The requested broader-minus-narrower order sets the sign of the response.')
heading('Derivative outputs and implementation')
p('Both directional responses and their combined magnitude are saved for Roberts, Prewitt, and Sobel. Opposite signs correspond to opposite intensity transitions; convolution reverses the sign relative to applying the same antisymmetric kernel as correlation. Magnitude is unchanged by this sign reversal.')
p('Functions used: numpy.pad, numpy.lib.stride_tricks.sliding_window_view, numpy.einsum, numpy.median, numpy.hypot, numpy.min/max, numpy.rint, numpy.clip, numpy.save, PIL.Image.open/fromarray/save. The actual kernels and custom convolve/normalize functions are in homework2.py.')

start('Problem 1 Directional edge responses')
grid([(f'{name.title()} {suffix}',R/'problem1'/f'{name}_{suffix}.png') for name in ['roberts','prewitt','sobel'] for suffix in ['x','y','magnitude']],side=125)
p('Each row shows the signed x response, signed y response, and combined gradient magnitude. Every panel is independently min-max normalized for visualization. Neutral interior signed responses appear near midgray, while unsigned magnitudes have a dark background. The raw arrays retain the actual scales for reproducibility.')
p('For the x response, differences across columns emphasize vertical scene boundaries. For the y response, differences across rows emphasize horizontal boundaries. Magnitude combines both directions and highlights contours independently of transition sign.')

start('Problem 2 Draw shapes')
p('The generated image follows the reference: square, circle, ellipse, and triangle from left to right; outlined versions above and filled versions below. It uses an 800 x 400 uint8 canvas with black background (0) and white shapes (255). Each square, circle, and triangle spans about 100 x 100 pixels; the ellipse has a 150 x 50 bounding box to match the elongated reference.')
c.drawImage(str(R/'problem2'/'shapes.png'),48,y-258,width=516,height=258);y-=280
p('The implementation constructs coordinate grids with numpy.indices and writes shape masks directly into the array. Squares use coordinate bounds. Circles and ellipses use ((x-cx)/rx)^2 + ((y-cy)/ry)^2 <= 1. Triangles use bounds and linear edge inequalities. Outlines subtract an inner region from the filled mask; the triangle outline removes pixels whose four adjacent neighbors are also inside.')
p('No prebuilt shape-drawing function is used. NumPy boolean indexing assigns all selected pixels at once. Slice endpoints follow Python convention: the upper bound is excluded, so a range that must include a last index requires last+1.')
p('Functions used: numpy.zeros, numpy.indices, absolute-value operations, boolean masks, NumPy slicing and indexing, and PIL.Image.fromarray/save. Custom function: problem2.')

start('Problem 3 Video and frame subtraction')
v=m['problem3']
if 'input_frames' not in v:
 p('Status: waiting for the student-created camera video. No capture details, motion observations, video frame counts, or codec size comparisons are claimed without that recording. The following describes the implemented method; the required outputs will be generated when the recording is supplied.')
else:
 p(f"Measured result: {v['input_frames']} input frames and {v['difference_frames']} difference frames, at 24 fps and 320 x 240. Both FFV1 outputs were decoded and verified pixel-for-pixel against their RGB PNG source sequences.")
 for name,sz in v['file_sizes_bytes'].items():p(f'{name}: {sz:,} bytes.')
p('Input: the supplied phone recording in data/input.mp4. It is 6.57 seconds long, contains H.264 video at nominal 30 fps and AAC audio, and has stored dimensions 1024 x 576. FFmpeg honors the orientation metadata; the displayed view is portrait. Only its first five seconds are processed.')
p('The Python pipeline calls FFmpeg to decode the first five seconds, preserve the aspect ratio while fitting within 320 x 240, pad to that canvas size, resample to 24 fps, and save RGB PNG frames. Resampling standardizes playback timing, rather than asserting that the original camera captured at 24 fps.')
p('The same canonical PNG sequence is encoded into original_msmpeg4v2.avi and original_ffv1.avi. The lossy encoder uses q:v=2 and yuv420p; the lossless encoder uses bgr0 to preserve RGB samples exactly. PNG frames are used for subtraction to avoid introducing lossy re-encoding noise before the calculation.')
p('For each successive pair, D[n] = clip(int16(I[n+1]) - int16(I[n]) + 128, 0, 255). Signed conversion before subtraction prevents uint8 wraparound. A pixel that does not change maps to 128; brighter changes map above 128 and darker changes below 128. Differences outside [-128,127] saturate as required.')
p('With N decoded images, there are N-1 differences. The handout example lists img00 through img99 (100 files) but also says 99 images; its stated 98 output frames applies only to 99 actual input files. The implementation uses the actual decoded count.')
p('The difference sequence is encoded into difference_msmpeg4v2.avi and difference_ffv1.avi at 24 fps. Audio is excluded. FFV1 preserves the already shifted and clipped difference frames; it cannot reverse information lost by the mandated clipping operation.')
p('The recording shows a hand and wristwatch moving in front of a keyboard and monitor. Hand and watch boundaries are visible as bright and dark differences. The camera background also shifts slightly, so keyboard and monitor edges respond; this is not a perfectly stationary capture. The method still reveals motion, but camera jitter and video noise prevent a clean isolated foreground blob.')
p('Functions used: pathlib.Path, subprocess.run, PIL.Image.open/convert, numpy.array/astype/clip, and custom frame_difference/problem3. FFmpeg performs decoding, scaling, frame-rate conversion and AVI encoding.')


start('Problem 3 Measured video results')
