c.drawImage(str(R/'problem3'/'contact_sheet.png'),48,y-210,width=516,height=210);y-=236
p('Original sequence samples appear above; shifted and clipped differences appear below. Unchanged pixels approach neutral gray (128); the black padding of the original frames also becomes gray in the difference images because its temporal difference is zero.')
p('The moving hand and watch give clear high-contrast contours. Around frame 101 the hand has left the view; residual background edges indicate slight camera movement and imaging variation. No denoising, stabilization, or thresholding is applied because this task requests the raw shifted frame differences.')
p('Original AVI: msmpeg4v2 578,072 bytes; FFV1 4,068,792 bytes (about 7.04 times larger). Difference AVI: msmpeg4v2 499,748 bytes; FFV1 3,588,626 bytes (about 7.18 times larger). The lossy files are smaller here; the comparison includes AVI container overhead and depends on these encoder settings.')
p('Both FFV1 videos were decoded back to RGB and matched every corresponding PNG pixel exactly. This verifies losslessness relative to the processed frames, not relative to the phone sensor: the uploaded phone video is already compressed, and resampling/scaling precede encoding.')
p('Required files: original_msmpeg4v2.avi, original_ffv1.avi, difference_msmpeg4v2.avi, and difference_ffv1.avi, all in results/problem3. Input and difference PNG sequences are included for reproducibility.')

start('Problem 4 Haar wavelet transform')
p('Each 2 x 2 block is represented by average A, horizontal H, vertical V, and diagonal D, using the exact normalization in the handout:')
for s in ['A = (I00 + I01 + I10 + I11) / 4','H = (I00 + I01 - I10 - I11) / 4','V = (I00 - I01 + I10 - I11) / 4','D = (I00 - I01 - I10 + I11) / 4']:p(s)
p('The image layout is [[A,H],[V,D]], following the reference diagram. Only A is transformed at the next level. The sizes of A after levels 0 through 8 are 256, 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, and 1 pixels on each side.')
p('All coefficients are float64. The three detail arrays at every level are retained in a Python list and saved in coefficients.npz; only the final 1 x 1 average is needed for reconstruction. Displayed details are shifted by 128 and clipped/rounded to uint8. Those display images are never used for reconstruction.')
p('Each saved layer image is a full-size 512 x 512 recursive mosaic. Previously produced detail bands remain in place as only the top-left average region is replaced by the next decomposition. Fine-scale differences are mostly near midgray, while strong structure survives into coarser layers.')
grid([(f'Level {i}',R/'problem4'/f'layer_{i:02d}.png') for i in range(3)],side=145)
p('Functions used: NumPy stride slicing, elementwise arithmetic, numpy.empty, numpy.savez, a Python list, PIL.Image.fromarray/save, and custom haar_layer/problem4.')

start('Problem 4 Remaining transform layers')
grid([(f'Level {i}',R/'problem4'/f'layer_{i:02d}.png') for i in range(3,9)],side=145)
p('The shrinking average block summarizes increasingly large regions. Detail bands retain contrasts at their respective scales. The final average is %.12f, equal to the mean intensity of the original image.'%m['problem4']['Apix'])
p('The decomposition rearranges image information into scale and orientation bands; by itself it does not reduce the number of coefficients. Many small detail coefficients can be coded efficiently, but discarding or quantizing them changes the reconstruction.')

start('Problem 4 Exact reconstruction')
for s in ['I00 = A + H + V + D','I01 = A + H - V - D','I10 = A - H + V - D','I11 = A - H - V + D']:p(s)
p('Reconstruction starts from Apix and consumes the saved detail triples in reverse level order, 8 down to 0. Every inverse step doubles the dimensions: 1 to 2 to 4 to 8 to 16 to 32 to 64 to 128 to 256 to 512. The handout I02 is interpreted as I10, the lower-left pixel.')
grid([('Original',R/'problem1'/'original.png'),('Reconstructed',R/'problem4'/'reconstructed.png'),('Absolute error',R/'problem4'/'absolute_error.png')],side=145)
p('Measured maximum absolute reconstruction error: 0.0. Mean absolute error: 0.0. Exactly equal pixels: 262,144 of 262,144. Error is calculated on the float64 reconstruction before uint8 conversion. The included assert enforces zero error.')
p('For this 8-bit input and nine stages, division by powers of two is represented exactly in float64 at the required precision. Therefore retaining every coefficient permits exact inversion. Rounding the coefficients to display images would destroy this guarantee.')
heading('Compression discussion')
p('Haar concentrates smooth content in averages and local changes in details. This supports multiresolution representation and efficient coding when details are small. Wavelet coding can reduce the block-boundary artifacts associated with heavily quantized 8 x 8 DCT JPEG, although performance depends on the image and coding method. Lossless coding also requires reversible processing and retention of all necessary coefficient information.')
p('Technical clarification: JPEG uses DCT, not DCE. JPEG2000 Part 1 uses reversible 5/3 or irreversible 9/7 wavelet filters, rather than the Haar filter implemented here. Haar demonstrates the multiscale principle; this experiment implements no JPEG2000 encoder and makes no measured compression-ratio claim.')
p('Reference: ITU-T T.803 (2024), JPEG2000 conformance testing, and JPEG committee overview: https://jpeg.org/jpeg2000/ .')
p('Verification: independent reference convolution comparisons, small-array Haar inversions, and signed-difference/clipping checks passed. The video pipeline was exercised using a separate synthetic test clip; that test is not submitted as a student camera recording.',size=9)
